= 1
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

A <phase diagram> partitions a space of control parameters into regions with distinct equilibrium phases. Its boundaries mark nonanalyticities of the thermodynamic-limit <free energy>; a <first-order phase transition> has a jump in a first derivative, whereas a <continuous phase transition> has a continuously vanishing <order parameter> and singular fluctuations.

An explicit three-parameter example is the scalar <Landau free energy>
$$
V(M;r,u,h)=\frac r2M^2+\frac u4M^4+\frac v6M^6-hM,\qquad v>0,
$$
with parameter axes $(r,u,h)$. These can be realized locally by the temperature, single-ion anisotropy and magnetic field of the <Blume–Capel model>. The three dimensions here refer to control-parameter space, not necessarily to three spatial dimensions. At $h=0$, minimizing this potential gives a <continuous phase transition> at $r=0$ for $u>0$. For $u<0$, coexistence occurs at $r=3u^2/(16v)$, where
$$
V(M)=\frac v6M^2\left(M^2+\frac{3u}{4v}\right)^2.
$$
There are three <global minima>, $M=0$ and $M=\pm\sqrt{-3u/(4v)}$. Their <order-parameter> jump decreases to zero as $u\uparrow0$. Thus **$(r,u,h)=(0,0,0)$ is a tricritical point.** The two transition curves in the $h=0$ plane meet there.

In the three-dimensional <phase diagram>, crossing $h=0$ inside the ordered region switches between opposite signs of $M$, so that region is itself a <first-order phase transition> sheet. For $u<0$, two <tricritical wings> at opposite nonzero fields separate weakly and strongly magnetized phases. Their <wing critical edges> can be obtained rather than merely sketched: require $V'=V''=V'''=0$. Since $V'''=6uM+20vM^3$, the nonzero solutions satisfy
$$
M_c^2=-\frac{3u}{10v},\qquad r_c=\frac{9u^2}{20v},\qquad h_c=\frac{6u^2M_c}{25v}.
$$
The fourth derivative there is $-12u>0$, giving ordinary continuous critical endpoints. The wings meet the zero-field coexistence sheet along the <tricritical three-phase line> and terminate at these edges. Their exact <tricritical wing coexistence factorization> supplies the surfaces in the figure: for two nonnegative minima $a\leq b$, set $s=a+b$, $p=ab$ and
$$
u=\frac{2v}{3}(-2s^2+3p),\quad r=\frac v3(s^4-s^2p+3p^2),\quad h=\frac v3s^3p.
$$
Direct expansion gives $V(M)-V(a)=(v/6)(M-a)^2(M-b)^2[(M+s)^2+p]\geq0$, proving that these surfaces describe coexistence of global, not merely local, minima.

\Image[paper-43-tricritical.png]
{title=Tricritical phase diagram of the sextic scalar free energy: first-order sheets, critical edges and the zero-field slice}

For the unheaded <Ising model> calculation, assume ferromagnetic exchange $J>0$ and let $M$ denote the mean <Ising spin>. On the hypercubic lattice the <coordination number of a lattice> is $z=2D$, even though the original positive-direction bond sum counts each bond only once. The <mean-field approximation> neglects products of deviations from $M$:
$$
\sigma_i\sigma_j\simeq M\sigma_i+M\sigma_j-M^2.
$$
There are $ND$ bonds, so the approximate energy is $H_{\rm MF}=-2DJM\sum_i\sigma_i+NDJM^2$. Each independent <Ising spin> therefore sees the effective field $2DJM$. With $\beta_{\rm th}=1/(k_BT)$, averaging its two Boltzmann weights gives the <self-consistency equation>
$$
\boxed{M=\frac{e^{2\beta_{\rm th}DJM}-e^{-2\beta_{\rm th}DJM}}{e^{2\beta_{\rm th}DJM}+e^{-2\beta_{\rm th}DJM}}
=\tanh\left(\frac{2DJM}{k_BT}\right).}
$$
The slope of its right side at zero is $T_c/T$, where
$$
\boxed{T_c=\frac{2DJ}{k_B}.}
$$
Above $T_c$, $\tanh x<x$ for $x>0$ excludes a nonzero solution. Below $T_c$ the initial slope exceeds one, and concavity of the hyperbolic tangent on the positive axis gives one positive solution and its negative partner. The <Ising auxiliary mean-field free energy> $DJM^2-k_BT\log[2\cosh(2\beta_{\rm th}DJM)]$ has positive quartic coefficient near $T_c$, while its quadratic coefficient changes sign there. Its stable minima therefore move continuously away from zero; this is a <continuous phase transition> in the <mean-field theory of the Ising model>.

Put $\tau=(T_c-T)/T_c>0$ and $c=T_c/T=(1-\tau)^{-1}$. The <Taylor expansion> $\tanh x=x-x^3/3+O(x^5)$ gives, on the nonzero branch,
$$
0=c-1-\frac{c^3}{3}M^2+O(M^4),\qquad
M^2=\frac{3(c-1)}{c^3}+O((c-1)^2)=3\tau+O(\tau^2).
$$
Consequently **$M\sim\sqrt{3}\,\tau^{1/2}$ and the mean-field order-parameter exponent is $\beta=1/2$.** The inverse-temperature symbol $\beta_{\rm th}$ used above is distinct from this <order-parameter critical exponent>.

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a ferromagnet the scalar <order parameter> is the spontaneous <spin magnetization>. It distinguishes a symmetry-invariant phase with zero mean from one of two phases with a nonzero mean of opposite signs. At zero field, a finite system with <spin inversion symmetry> has exactly zero mean. The spontaneous value is defined by taking the <thermodynamic limit> first and then sending the symmetry-breaking <conjugate field> to zero from one side.

A <Landau-Ginzburg theory> promotes the coarse magnetization to a scalar field $M(x)$ and uses
$$
\mathcal F[M]=\int d^Dx\left[\frac\kappa2|\nabla M|^2+\frac r2M^2+\frac u4M^4+\frac v6M^6-hM\right],\qquad \kappa>0,\ v>0.
$$
At $h=0$, <spin inversion symmetry> permits only even local powers. The gradient term penalizes spatial variations. The equilibrium statistical weight is $e^{-\mathcal F/(k_BT)}$; the <Landau approximation> chooses a stable minimum of $\mathcal F$, whereas the full functional integral retains fluctuations around it. In a homogeneous phase this gives the <equation of state> $h=rM+uM^3+vM^5$. The field $h$ is conjugate to the <order parameter>, with $M=-\partial f_{\rm eq}/\partial h$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <connected correlation function> $G(x)=\langle M(x)M(0)\rangle-\langle M\rangle^2$, since the disconnected constant in an ordered phase does not measure fluctuation decay. Away from criticality its large-distance exponential defines the <correlation length>:
$$
\xi^{-1}=-\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac1r\log|G(r)|,
$$
when that limit exists. Algebraic prefactors do not affect this definition.

Expand the <Landau-Ginzburg theory> about a stable homogeneous minimum $M_0$. With $A=V''(M_0)>0$, the quadratic fluctuation energy is $\frac12\int d^Dx[\kappa|\nabla\delta M|^2+A(\delta M)^2]$. The <Gaussian functional integral> gives
$$
\widetilde G(k)=\frac{k_BT}{\kappa k^2+A},\qquad \boxed{\xi=\sqrt{\kappa/A}.}
$$
This <Ornstein--Zernike correlation function> shows why the curvature of the free-energy minimum controls correlations. At a <continuous phase transition> that curvature vanishes, allowing long-range fluctuations. In the ordinary quartic theory with $u>0$, $A=r$ above the transition and $A=-2r$ on either ordered branch below, so $\xi\propto|r|^{-1/2}$ on both sides. At an equilibrium first-order transition the competing minima generally retain nonzero curvature, and the <correlation length> remains finite. The limit of local stability at a <spinodal point> is a distinct mean-field phenomenon.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Microscopically different systems can have the same <critical exponents> and appropriately normalized scaling functions. This universality means that they belong to one <universality class> when coarse-graining brings them to the same <renormalization-group fixed point>. Spatial dimension, <order-parameter> symmetry and the range of the interactions affect that classification; changing a lattice spacing or a microscopic coupling generally changes nonuniversal amplitudes and the critical temperature instead.

In the scalar short-range <Landau-Ginzburg theory>, a positive quartic term and one thermal tuning produce ordinary Ising-type criticality. A generic sextic term does not change that fixed point, but tuning the quartic coefficient to zero exposes a different <tricritical point> with different exponents. The <mean-field approximation> itself neglects fluctuations. Its numerical exponents are reliable only when the <Ginzburg criterion> makes those fluctuations subleading; the ordinary scalar <upper critical dimension> is four, while the tricritical one is three. At an upper critical dimension logarithmic corrections can remain.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $h=0$ in the even sextic <Landau free energy>. For $u>0$, near $r=0$ the nonzero stationary branch satisfies $M^2=-r/u+O(r^2)$ for $r<0$. The minimum value is $V_{\min}=-r^2/(4u)+O(r^3)$ below the transition and zero above. The <order parameter> is continuous, and if $r$ crosses zero linearly with temperature, the first temperature derivative of the minimized <free-energy density> is continuous too. There is no <latent heat>; a second derivative has a finite jump at this level.

For $u<0$, solve the two conditions $V'(M)=0$ and $V(M)=V(0)$ with $M\ne0$. Setting $q=M^2$ gives $r+uq+vq^2=0$ and $r/2+uq/4+vq^2/6=0$. Eliminating $r$ gives
$$
\boxed{M^2=-\frac{3u}{4v},\qquad r=\frac{3u^2}{16v}.}
$$
Both the disordered and ordered minima have positive curvature there, but their values coincide. Crossing this <phase coexistence> boundary changes the <global minimum> discontinuously. The <order parameter> jumps; for $r=r(T)$ at fixed $u,v$, $\partial_TV_{\min}=r'(T)M^2/2$ jumps as well, generically yielding a nonzero <latent heat>. Local extrema can persist past coexistence as <metastable> states, which explains hysteresis in a dynamically delayed transition. The <spinodal points>, where local curvature vanishes, do not locate the equilibrium transition.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The first-order coexistence curve approaches the continuous curve as $u\uparrow0$: both meet at $r=u=h=0$. To obtain this <tricritical point>, two even coefficients must be tuned to zero, while the sextic coefficient remains positive. A nonzero <conjugate field> removes the zero-field inversion symmetry and leads to the wing structure described in the root solution.

At $u=h=0$, the <equation of state> becomes $0=rM+vM^5$. For $r<0$,
$$
M^4=-r/v,\qquad V_{\min}=-\frac{|r|^{3/2}}{3\sqrt v},\qquad V''(M)=4|r|.
$$
Above the transition $M=0$ and $V''(0)=r$. These are stable branches of a bounded potential, and the ordered value tends continuously to zero at $r=0$, but with $M\propto(-r)^{1/4}$ rather than the ordinary square-root law. **Tricritical behavior requires $r=u=0$, $h=0$ and $v>0$; a generic path with fixed $u>0$ instead has ordinary critical behavior.**

= f
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

A <critical exponent> is the leading power of a singular observable as a control variable approaches a critical value. Use $r\propto t=(T-T_c)/T_c$ and distinguish the exponents $\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\nu,\eta$ from coefficient names in a Hamiltonian. Their standard definitions are
$$
M\sim(-t)^\beta,\quad \chi\sim|t|^{-\gamma},\quad C_s\sim|t|^{-\alpha},\quad
M(t=0)\sim\operatorname{sgn}(h)|h|^{1/\delta},\quad
\xi\sim|t|^{-\nu},\quad G(r;t=0)\sim r^{-(D-2+\eta)}.
$$
The <Landau approximation> predicts these powers by minimizing the <Landau free energy> and differentiating the <equation of state>. At an ordinary quartic point, $M^2=-r/u$, $\chi^{-1}=r$ above and $2|r|$ below, and $h=uM^3$ on the critical isotherm. The equilibrium singular free energy is proportional to $-r^2$ below, giving a heat-capacity jump. The quadratic fluctuation denominator is $\kappa k^2+V''$, so $\nu=1/2$ and $\eta=0$. Thus the ordinary <mean-field critical exponents> are
$$
\boxed{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\nu,\eta)=(0,\tfrac12,1,3,\tfrac12,0).}
$$
At a tuned tricritical point, the preceding solution gives $M\propto(-r)^{1/4}$, $V_{\min}\propto-|r|^{3/2}$ and $\chi^{-1}=4|r|$ below. At $r=0$ the <equation of state> is $h=vM^5$. The curvature still vanishes linearly, and the Gaussian gradient term still gives zero <anomalous dimension>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta,\nu,\eta)=(\tfrac12,\tfrac14,1,5,\tfrac12,0).}
$$
These <mean-field tricritical exponent calculations> require approach along the tuned quartic-zero direction. Fluctuations modify the powers below the appropriate <upper critical dimension>, and marginal effects can add logarithms at it.

= 2
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Take the <connected correlation function>
$$
G(r)=\langle\sigma_0\sigma_r\rangle-\langle\sigma_0\rangle\langle\sigma_r\rangle.
$$
For a massive phase its large-distance form is an algebraic prefactor times $e^{-|r|/\xi}$, defining the exponential <correlation length> $\xi$. To specify the normalization of <magnetic susceptibility>, add $-h\sum_r\sigma_r$ to the energy and differentiate the mean spin. Translation invariance gives the <correlation-function susceptibility sum rule>
$$
\boxed{\chi=\frac{\partial M}{\partial h}=\frac{\beta_{\rm th}}N\operatorname{Var}\left(\sum_r\sigma_r\right)=\beta_{\rm th}\sum_rG(r),\qquad \beta_{\rm th}=(k_BT)^{-1}.}
$$
Using the dimensionless source $\beta_{\rm th}h$ removes the prefactor $\beta_{\rm th}$. Below the transition, use a selected pure phase rather than a mixture of the two magnetizations, which would have a macroscopic disconnected fluctuation.

Choose a nonnegative <blocking kernel> $K_b(\sigma',\sigma)$ normalized by $\sum_{\sigma'}K_b(\sigma',\sigma)=1$. It may enforce a block average, a majority rule or a chosen rescaled coarse spin. Define the transformed weight by
$$
e^{-\beta_{\rm th}[H(u',\sigma')+N'C']}=
\sum_\sigma K_b(\sigma',\sigma)e^{-\beta_{\rm th}[H(u,\sigma)+NC]}.
$$
Summing this identity over coarse spins shows exact preservation of the <partition function>. The geometrical changes are **$a'=ba$ and $N'=N/b^D$**. A coordinate rescaling subsequently restores the reference cutoff if desired. The transformation is exact only if all generated operators are retained; a finite-coupling truncation is an approximation.

Define the coupling-independent term generated when $C=0$ by
$$
\sum_\sigma K_b(\sigma',\sigma)e^{-\beta_{\rm th}H(u,\sigma)}
=e^{-\beta_{\rm th}[H(R_bu,\sigma')+N'c(u)]}.
$$
Restoring the original $C$ gives the <identity-operator contribution to renormalization-group free energy>:
$$
\boxed{u_{p+1}=R_b(u_p),\qquad C_{p+1}=b^DC_p+c(u_p).}
$$
The parameter $C_p$ is an accumulated additive energy per coarse site. It multiplies the identity operator and leaves normalized spin probabilities unchanged, but it must be kept when calculating the absolute <free energy>. It records the eliminated-mode entropy and the normalization of their statistical measure.

Use an energy-per-site convention: $F(u,C)=-\log Z(u,C,N)/(\beta_{\rm th}N)$ and $f(u)=F(u,0)$, so $F=f+C$. Partition-function invariance gives
$$
F(u_p,C_p)=b^{-D}F(u_{p+1},C_{p+1}).
$$
Consequently, with $g(u)=b^{-D}c(u)$,
$$
f(u)=b^{-D}f(R_bu)+g(u),\qquad
\boxed{f(u_0)=b^{-pD}f(u_p)+\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}b^{-jD}g(u_j).}
$$
This is the <additive free-energy recursion under blocking>. In a dimensionless free-energy convention multiply $f,C,c,g$ by $\beta_{\rm th}$; the same recursion holds. The inhomogeneous term comes from the generated identity operator, not from a change in the spin-dependent couplings alone.

The affine equation naturally applies to the unsubtracted free energy. To extract its singular part, write $f=f_{\rm reg}+F_s$ and choose a regular background satisfying $f_{\rm reg}(u)=b^{-D}f_{\rm reg}(R_bu)+g(u)$. Subtraction then gives $F_s(u)=b^{-D}F_s(R_bu)$. This <analytic subtraction of an inhomogeneous renormalization recursion> is justified when the eliminated finite-shell contribution is analytic near the fixed point and admits a regular subtraction. It can fail in a resonant direction, producing additive logarithms. The simple homogeneous formulas also assume the remaining <irrelevant operators> are not <dangerously irrelevant couplings>, and that <marginal operators> do not produce unaccounted logarithmic corrections. Calling the quantity in the affine equation “singular” does not make $g$ vanish automatically.

Near a <renormalization-group fixed point> $u_*$, linearize the coupling map. In scaling coordinates $v_i$, write $v_i'=b^{\lambda_i}v_i$ to leading order. Here $b^{\lambda_i}$ is a discrete multiplier, whereas $\lambda_i$ is its logarithmic scaling exponent. A <relevant operator> has $\lambda_i>0$ and grows under coarse-graining; an <irrelevant operator> has $\lambda_i<0$ and decays. A <marginal operator> needs nonlinear analysis. Tuning the relevant fields to zero places the system on the <critical surface>. At the fixed point the spin field is rescaled as $\sigma'(x')=b^{\Delta_\sigma}\sigma_<(bx')$ with $\Delta_\sigma=(D-2+\eta)/2$. Its conjugate source therefore has $\lambda_h=D-\Delta_\sigma=(D+2-\eta)/2$. This accounts for both coupling and spin rescaling; the field normalization is part of the transformation, not a change in the physical microscopic spin length.

Assume the thermal field $t=(T-T_c)/T_c$ and magnetic field $h$ are the only remaining relevant directions. The homogeneous <scaling hypothesis for critical phenomena> gives
$$
F_s(t,h)=b^{-pD}F_s(b^{p\lambda_t}t,b^{p\lambda_h}h).
$$
Choose the stopping scale with $b^{p\lambda_t}|t|=1$. Then
$$
\boxed{F_s(t,h)=|t|^{D/\lambda_t}f_\pm\left(h/|t|^{\lambda_h/\lambda_t}\right),}
$$
where **$f_+$ is the branch for $t>0$ and $f_-$ the branch for $t<0$**. Nonuniversal metric factors can be absorbed into the definitions of $t,h$ and these functions. Correlation lengths transform as $\xi(t)=b^p\xi(b^{p\lambda_t}t)$, so $\nu=1/\lambda_t$. Differentiating the singular free energy twice with respect to temperature gives $C_s\propto|t|^{D/\lambda_t-2}$, and hence the <hyperscaling relation>
$$
\boxed{\alpha=2-D\nu.}
$$
Two source derivatives similarly give $\gamma=(2\lambda_h-D)/\lambda_t$. A single source derivative gives the ordered-branch exponent $\beta=(D-\lambda_h)/\lambda_t$, and setting $t=0$ and scaling $h$ to one gives $\delta=\lambda_h/(D-\lambda_h)$ when the corresponding magnetization branch exists. These show explicitly how the <critical exponents> follow from the fixed-point scaling eigenvalues.

The independent correlation derivation uses $G(r)=A r^{-(D-2+\eta)}f_G(r/\xi)$ with a single crossover scaling function. For lattice spacing $a$, the singular continuum contribution to the <magnetic susceptibility> is
$$
\chi_s\simeq\beta_{\rm th}a^{-D}A S_D\int_a^\infty r^{1-\eta}f_G(r/\xi)\,dr
\sim\beta_{\rm th}a^{-D}A S_D\xi^{2-\eta}\int_0^\infty s^{1-\eta}f_G(s)\,ds.
$$
Here $S_D$ is the area of the unit $(D-1)$-sphere. The final integral is finite and nonzero when $\eta<2$, $f_G(0)$ is finite and nonzero, and the tail decays exponentially. Microscopic distances contribute a regular background. With $\xi\sim|t|^{-\nu}$ this proves the <Fisher scaling relation>
$$
\boxed{\chi_s\propto\xi^{2-\eta},\qquad \gamma=(2-\eta)\nu.}
$$
There is an amplitude qualification in the printed large-distance formula: its tail has the Gaussian shape and, taken literally with amplitude independent of $\xi$, integrates to a term proportional to $\xi^2$. For a nonzero anomalous exponent it must instead be matched to the critical short-distance normalization. The <matching the amplitude of a massive critical correlation tail> gives
$$
G(r)\sim A_\infty\,\xi^{-\eta}\frac{\xi}{(\xi r)^{(D-1)/2}}e^{-r/\xi},\qquad r\gg\xi.
$$
At $r=s\xi$ this has precisely the same overall $\xi^{-(D-2+\eta)}$ scaling as the critical form. Thus the requested identity uses the full scaling hypothesis, or equivalently this implicit amplitude factor. The printed tail without it is consistent as written in the Gaussian case $\eta=0$.

